# Match the js node because over Bluetooth the controller advertises enough keys that udev tags it a keyboard, not a
# joystick. A controller unlocks the desktop, so only the paired ones listed match: over Bluetooth (bus 0005) the kernel
# sets uniq to the bonded address, while a USB device could claim any vendor ID or serial.
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="js*", ATTRS{id/bustype}=="0005", ATTRS{id/vendor}=="@XBOX_VENDOR@", \
    ATTRS{uniq}=="@XBOX_CONTROLLERS@", \
    TAG+="systemd", ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="steam-tv.service", ENV{STEAM_TV_CONTROLLER}="1"
# A removed device has no attributes left to match, but udev keeps the properties set when it was added.
# SYSTEMD_WANTS acts only on add, so start the unit directly.
ACTION=="remove", SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="js*", ENV{STEAM_TV_CONTROLLER}=="1", \
    RUN+="/usr/bin/systemctl --no-block start steam-tv-off.service"
